// Before the agent acknowledged the deployment's command the deployment ends as `canceled` at once. Once the agent runs it, a cancel request is sent to the agent (`cancelRequestedAt` is set) and the deployment ends as `canceled` when the agent reports back, with the step it reached in `canceledAtStep`: a pull or build is stopped (a build's work directory is removed), containers of the new release are removed and the current release keeps serving, during verification the previous routes are restored first, and a recreate deploy that already stopped the previous release starts it again. If the previous release cannot be restored the result is `rollback_failed` with manual recovery steps. Once the new release is live it is too late: the deployment finishes (a still-running post-deploy command is stopped). An offline server receives the request when it reconnects. A finished deployment is 409 `cannot_cancel`.
